[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1174","stub":{"id":"1174","section":"1. Mascoma Lake to Route 4, Lebanon","altname":"","river":"Mascoma","difficulty":"IItoIIIstandoutIV","states":["USA-NHM"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":null,"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":null,"id":"109369","location":{"latitude":"43.647999","longitude":"-72.1819"},"name":"Put In","distance":"0","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":null,"id":"114610","location":{"latitude":"43.639203","longitude":"-72.23376"},"name":"Take Out","distance":"3.9","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[{"id":"101248576","title":null,"post_date":"2011-09-05 04:00:00+00","detail":"On August 28th, 2011 Hurricane Irene struck New England. The resulting floods caused extensive damage throughout the region, the worst in over 100 years. More than half the rivers in Vermont and northern New Hampshire recorded their highest flow levels ever. Many roads, guardrails, power lines, bridges, trees and other debris now litter several rivers throughout the region. River beds have been scoured and changed course, many new strainers make navigation problematic at best and downright dangerous at worse. Please realize that the river description you see here may not match current situation after the floods. Use common sense and when in doubt scout especially on blind drops. Also, if you run this river in the next year or so please comment on its navigability, even if there are no problems this will be very helpful. Please report any new strainers or changes to the rapids that will impact future boating.\nThank you,","user":{"name":"Mark Lacroix"}},{"id":"101247884","title":null,"post_date":"2011-04-13 04:00:00+00","detail":"The high waters on the Scoma right now mean some of the slalom course wires set up at lower water may become a hazard. Definitely scout the slalom course stretch under the covered bridge before running it at these high levels.","user":{"name":"Chris Heim"}},{"id":"101247847","title":null,"post_date":"2011-04-04 04:00:00+00","detail":"As of 4/3/11, there's dangerous wood on the right after the last rapid (Excelsior). It can be skirted on the left or the right, but for beginners or swimmers it could be dangerous. Be careful.","user":{"name":"Gregory Sokol"}}],"photos":[{"id":"889769","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"892277","description":"Decent amount of old metal objects throughout the run. Not bad, visible. Some bars and things that are not as obvious. Pretty standard stuff for NE Mill Country rivers.","caption":"Antique Store","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Brad Walker","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/889769","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/889769"}},{"id":"889767","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"892275","description":"Overflow section of dam only had 1-2\"  - see my river Comment 8/12/2018 from 8/7/2018 run for details.","caption":"Dam overflow ","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Brad Walker","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/889767","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/889767"}},{"id":"889772","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"892280","description":"At low flow gotta go Right by the island. At higher flows, it looks like there are a few channels to choose from with potential for some fun play.","caption":"Island Split","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Brad Walker","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/889772","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/889772"}},{"id":"889771","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"892279","description":"Higher flow could make for some play features in a handful of sections like this one.","caption":"Little Boulders","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Brad Walker","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/889771","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/889771"}},{"id":"889768","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"892276","description":"This is the release section of the dam. Not much coming out that way.","caption":"Dam Release","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Brad Walker","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/889768","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/889768"}},{"id":"889770","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"892278","description":"Your non-paddlers can enjoy themselves on Lake Mascoma proper, or hit the criss-crossing Rail Trail and still feel like you're participating in family events as you go!","caption":"Non-Paddler Friendly Run","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Brad Walker","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/889770","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/889770"}}],"detail":{"id":"1174","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"This section is listed in Let it Rain.\n\nDrawdowns of Mascoma Lake likely make this section runnable in the fall. For some hairy stuff, check out the [Downtown](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/rivers/id/3243/) section.\n\nThe United State Geologic Survey discontinued reporting flow and stage levels at their gage station located at the Mascoma Lake out flow.  The New Hampshire Department of Environmental Services took over the gage shortly thereafter and started posting information at their site.  [Click Here](http://www2.des.state.nh.us/rti_home/station_information_display.asp?WID=fivebasins&ID=MCAN3&NAME=Mascoma+Lake).\n\nThe only reason for a maximum level in is because of strainers on the banks. The actual difficulty of the whitewater does not change much (just a little pushier and fewer rocks).\n\nThere is a [gauge located in another branch of this system](https://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/Gauge2/detail/id/30657/) which could be used for correlation. However, there is no 75th percentile or paddler observations to base any correlation off of. If you run this section please check the online gauge and leave a comment with the flow and a description of the run.","averageGradient":null,"maxGradient":null,"length":"3.5","correlations":[],"pointOfInterestIDs":["109369","114610"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-72.18182873173299,43.648579598915376],[-72.18230933173226,43.6486753989152],[-72.1825193983986,43.64873713224847],[-72.18264833173174,43.648812332248326],[-72.1830239983978,43.64908239891457],[-72.1832347317308,43.64925453224765],[-72.18369333173013,43.64958813224712],[-72.18401313172961,43.649946398913244],[-72.18418933172933,43.65008179891305],[-72.1844541317289,43.65024619891278],[-72.1847775317284,43.65033773224599],[-72.1851077983946,43.65035153224596],[-72.18519513172777,43.65033439891266],[-72.18530013172762,43.65029133224607],[-72.18557379839388,43.650091198913],[-72.18592373172663,43.64988293224667],[-72.1861847983929,43.64962759891375],[-72.18647699839244,43.64930773224757],[-72.18703739839158,43.64849399891551],[-72.18744599839096,43.64812733224943],[-72.18769473172392,43.64792519891637],[-72.18876779838888,43.64674513225157],[-72.18887053172205,43.64665033225168],[-72.18912199838837,43.64647639891865],[-72.18954259838767,43.646354932252166],[-72.18965553172086,43.64629999891889],[-72.18977053172068,43.646198332252425],[-72.18987553172053,43.64602919891934],[-72.18993693172042,43.64544939892022],[-72.18999959838698,43.64513939892072],[-72.19004933172022,43.644505132255006],[-72.19002413172029,43.644419398921855],[-72.1898255317206,43.6441245322556],[-72.18972733172075,43.643928732255915],[-72.18969739838747,43.64379919892281],[-72.18973839838736,43.64365973225637],[-72.18991893172